    "lawCode": "Amd §1201, 1202 & 1209, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A1005C\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to revis-\ning the standards relating to the incorporation of stock or mutual\ninsurance companies\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL: The purpose of this bill is to revise\nthe standards relating to the incorporation of stock and/or mutual\ninsurance companies in New York so that the minimum board size can be\nreduced to seven, from the current minimum size of thirteen for large\ncompanies and nine for small companies for both mutual and stock compa-\nnies; the NYS residency requirement is reduced from two to one director;\nthe incorporators of an insurance corporation need only list their city\nand state of residence in newspapers and the company charter; and mutual\ninsurers can reduce the requirement for at least two principal officers\nas members of the board to at least one principal officer.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS: This bill would amend §1201(a)(3) of the\ninsurance law to remove the requirement that the proposed incorporators\nof a New York insurance corporation must publish in a newspaper their\nresidence address and require, instead, that they need only publish\ntheir city and state of residence. Section 1201 (a)(5)(B)(v), (vi) &\n(vii) of the insurance law would be amended to:  reduce the minimum size\nof the Board of Directors for insurance corporations in New York to\nseven; to reduce the New York State residency requirement for insurance\ncorporations in New York from two to one director; and to require that\nonly the city and state of residence of the incorporators of an insur-\nance corporation must be contained in the corporate charter. This bill\nwould also amend §1202(a)(2) of the insurance law to make consistent the\nreduced minimum board size of seven and clarify the rules for increasing\nor decreasing the number of board members on insurance corporations in\nNew York. Lastly, §1209(b) &(c) of the insurance law is being amended to\nauthorize a minimum board size of seven for mutual insurance corpo-\nrations in New York and to reduce the requisite number of principal\nofficers (inside directors) required for such corporations from the\ncurrent two to one.\n\nJUSTIFICATION: The requirement that the incorporators of a new insurance\ncorporation in New York must publicize their home addresses has been a\npotential impediment to the formation of new companies in the state. It\nis understandable that these individuals would be uncomfortable with\ndisclosing their home addresses in the newspaper and that information is\nnot essential for the public to know.  Publication of the city and state\nof residence would seem sufficient for this purpose.\n\nLikewise, there seems to be no rationale for requiring that at least two\nof the board members must be New York state residents or that the mini-\nmum board size must be thirteen, especially when the average board size\nin corporate America is approximately ten. Many insurers in New York are\nnow global enterprises and, given the increased demands for improved\ncorporate governance being placed upon financial services corporations,\nit does not seem necessary to require that two directors must be state\n\nresidents or that they must have an overly-large board of directors.\nThe primary consideration for selecting members of a company's Board of\nDirectors should be to identify individuals with superior intelligence\nand high values who have an understanding of the business at hand,\nregardless of their location or size.\n\nEXISTING LAW: Section 1201 (a)(3) and (5) of the insurance law currently\nrequire that the proposed incorporators of an insurance corporation in\nNew York must publish their resident addresses in the newspaper and in\nthe company charter. Sections 1201 (a)(5)(B)(v), 1202(a)(2), and 1209(b)\nof the insurance law require that the minimum size of insurance corpo-\nration boards of directors in New York must be thirteen, unless the\ncorporation meets a small company threshold, in which case the minimum\nboard size will be nine. Section 1201 (a)(5)(B)(vi) of the insurance law\ncurrently requires that at least two of the members of the boards of\ndirectors of insurance corporations in New York must be New York State\nresidents. Lastly, § 1209(b) of the insurance law requires that mutual\ninsurance corporations in New York must have at least two principal\nofficers (inside directors) on their Board of Directors.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  2007-08: A8767 Referred to Insurance/S.2085-A\nPassed Senate.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act will take effect immediately.\n\n",
